#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573} :activity/id #uuid "0a27c7be-9281-5ee1-a624-d9ff46333578" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438 :topic/id #uuid "493479ab-e6ae-52f5-9c1a-827aa0443283" :topic/math-academy-id 1359} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107065 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8dec747f86d6b276431ef02675d3827028508be9aa9ca3699ca4e6bcd6c.png)\n\nThe next step is to identify where the function is increasing and decreasing. Below, the intervals where $f(x)$ is increasing are shown in blue, and the intervals where $f(x)$ is decreasing are shown in green.\n\n![](images/10/10edc10cefa3cc48713795e0e2f40b05ba6174e2da7aa5698a02fc35444b1c45.png)\n\nSince we know where the function is increasing or decreasing, we can determine where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative.\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are positive, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)>0.\n$$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are negative, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)<0.\n$$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be  summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,2) & 2 & (2,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{'} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- $f'(x)>0$ on $(-\\infty,2) \\cup (4,+\\infty),$ while\n\n- $f'(x)<0$ on $(2,4).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a75bc2f2-f4fa-50c8-9eaa-230212f35132" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2661 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "136ca6f2-4f48-519a-8e2b-fcd52eaff347" :step/math-academy-id 12776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105566 :question/id #uuid "6b6ba1b8-5041-57c3-bcbb-239af0faa4dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341972}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"} :step/id #uuid "fe83e38b-e03f-5af9-907e-72d9f01b87e5" :step/math-academy-id 12783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107067 :tutorial/content "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing, we can calculate the derivative and then solve the appropriate inequality.\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9bdb8755-6e15-5d0b-b4da-b1a720b6dc4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2665 :tutorial/title "Calculating Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "bff9aeb3-19b2-547d-b422-3a3680105413" :step/math-academy-id 12785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105568 :question/id #uuid "7be416a1-e729-58a4-bc3c-d622d058eba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400667}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "10ad6cd4-75ee-5a9e-9c37-f75681712cd6" :step/math-academy-id 12790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105571 :question/id #uuid "cc1f0cb5-5cca-5a70-a89f-2983a64e273f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402991}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "371b12ac-a46c-5fdd-8712-331bed644307" :step/math-academy-id 12793 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105572 :question/id #uuid "88cdcba5-2572-5a6a-aa99-826b80cb1d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-3734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106361} {:db/id #ed